1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,000
Liverpool v Man City, 86th minute, all square.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:08,400
Szoboszlai shoots. WHYLINE freezes the real frame and replays the moment 200 times.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:11,900
Blocked. The pass to Salah was worth 2 more points of win chance.

4
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:16,800
Man Utd attack. The runner in the six-yard box is worth 28%; shooting now, 3.2%.

5
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:22,900
The ball is lost. Van de Ven takes it, and the speed gun follows him through the camera cuts.

6
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:38,900
37.0 km/h at the top, at least 91 m end to end.

7
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:44,900
Every line is drawn on the real pitch, through the broadcast camera.

8
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:50,000
Eze shot. Going back to Saka was the better ball.

9
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:54,200
A refereeing call, explained from the tracking: the run, the contact, the closing speed.

10
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,300
An assessment, not a ruling: about 15 km/h into the challenge, outside the box.

11
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:02,000
Arsenal v Chelsea, stoppage time: a shot from 4.5 m.

12
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:06,200
Where Raya stood he covers 39% of the goal. 1.8 m further out, all of it.

13
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:09,800
Man Utd v Man City: one second before Dorgu scores.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,800
City's defender needed 6.5 m and could cover 7.0. There, the goal chance falls from 26% to 19%.

15
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:22,100
The same intelligence becomes an analyst's breakdown, a casual fan's line, and a player's story.

16
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:28,100
Measured on this footage: 99.3% of players found, graphics within 1.5 px of the painted lines.

17
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:37,000
How it ended: Liverpool led until Silva's equaliser, then City scored twice more.

